esult schema prototype | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n| E003 | Benchmark dataset licensing analysis | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n| BRANCH-A-E004 | Extend LMEvalJob into supported evaluation execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n| BRANCH-B-E004 | New platform evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n| E005 | Model Registry result-persistence validation | Implementation | Model Registry | Model Registry | P0 |\n| E006 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform evaluation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n| E007 | Dashboard evaluation views | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n| E008 | Evaluation pipeline step | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | P1 |\n| E009 | Evaluation workflow product documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P0* |\n\n*P0 per docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority: Critical → P0). Not priority inheritance.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\nMermaid with branch nodes dashed.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\nEdges table with rules.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\n## Branch documentation\n\n---\n\nNow let me write out each epic body.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\nTitle: Investigation: Evaluation execution engine architecture\n\nDescription: Determine whether the platform's evaluation execution path should build on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) as the single execution path, or whether a new evaluation service is required that can also back Llama Stack's eval provider API. Deliverable: a written technical recommendation (ADR-style) with evidence.\n\nScope:\n- Gap analysis of LMEvalJob (Tech Preview) against the strategy's requirements: targeting KServe endpoints and Llama Stack distributions as evaluation targets, standard + custom benchmark suites, result persistence to Model Registry, cost/duration metadata capture.\n- Analysis of Llama Stack's upstream eval provider API contract and what a platform-backed provider requires.\n- Load/capacity and lifecycle considerations of running benchmark Jobs per project.\n- Recommendation: single execution path vs new service, with migration/promotion implications (Tech Preview → supported).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- A written recommendation documents LMEvalJob gaps vs requirements, with a go/no-go on extending it as the single execution path.\n- The Llama Stack eval provider API requirements are characterized (interface, semantics) sufficiently to design a provider against the chosen engine.\n- Bounded outcome recorded: Outcome A (extend LMEvalJob) or Outcome B (new evaluation service), each with the downstream implementation shape it implies.\n- If a new standalone repository would be required for Outcome B, the recommendation says so explicitly (it would trigger a repo/pipeline onboarding chain).\n\nHLR Traceability: Enables HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-7 (gating decision; no direct implementation).\n\nInvestigation signals for E001: as decided above.\n\n**E002 body:**\n\nTitle: Investigation: Canonical evaluation result schema\n\nDescription: Prototype and commit the canonical evaluation result format persisted to Model Registry custom properties, validated against real lm-evaluation-harness (MMLU) output before committing.\n\nScope:\n- Run real lm-eval benchmark execution (e.g., MMLU subset) and capture actual output shapes.\n- Design the canonical result schema: per-benchmark scores, run metadata (cost/duration fields: tokens, wall-clock, GPU time), suite/task identity and versions.\n- Prototype persistence to Model Registry ModelVersion custom properties via the REST API; verify round-trip fidelity.\n- Verify schema resilience to benchmark-suite version bumps (versioned suite identity, additive field strategy).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Schema document produced covering scores, suite/task/version identity, and cost/duration metadata (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time).\n- Validated against real MMLU output (not synthetic).\n- Demonstrated round-trip through Model Registry custom properties.\n- Version-bump resilience demonstrated or explicit mitigation documented.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-3, HLR-8 (schema definition); enables HLR-5 display.\n\n**E003 body:**\n\nTitle: Investigation: Benchmark dataset licensing and redistribution\n\nDescription: Determine which standard benchmark suites can be redistributed by Red Hat in disconnected environments and which must be fetched by the customer, producing a per-suite delivery decision.\n\nScope:\n- Compile license terms for candidate standard suites exposed via lm-eval task names (e.g., MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag).\n- Determine redistribution rights for product shipping in disconnected/air-gapped environments.\n- Per-suite delivery mechanism: bundle in product vs documented customer fetch.\n- Identify any legal sign-off needed for the redistribution allowlist.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- Per-suite allowlist: redistributable vs customer-fetched, with license evidence (license text references).\n- Legal confirmation obtained for suites marked redistributable (or explicit conditionality recorded).\n- Delivery mechanism per suite documented for the execution implementation.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-2 (gating out-of-the-box suite delivery).\n\n**BRANCH-A-E004 body:**\n\nTitle: Extend LMEvalJob into the supported evaluation execution path (Outcome A)\n\nDescription: If the execution-engine investigation recommends building on TrustyAI's LMEvalJob, extend it from Tech Preview into the supported platform evaluation capability: launch evaluations against platform-served models, ship standard suites out of the box, support custom tasks, record cost metadata, and persist results to Model Registry.\n\nScope:\n- Extend LMEvalJob to target models served on the platform (KServe InferenceService endpoints and Llama Stack distributions) as evaluation targets.\n- Standard benchmark suite delivery out of the box per the licensing allowlist (bundled suites vs documented customer fetch).\n- Custom evaluation task support: user-supplied datasets and metrics run through the same flow.\n- Cost/duration metadata capture per run (tokens, wall-clock, GPU time) per canonical schema.\n- Persist results to the Model Registry version of the evaluated model using the canonical result schema (REST API custom properties).\n- Promotion of the capability from Tech Preview to supported (stability, docs of CRD fields, upgrade path).\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- A user can launch an evaluation of a model served on the platform (KServe endpoint or Llama Stack distribution) against a selected benchmark suite.\n- Standard suites (e.g., MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag via lm-eval task names) are available out of the box per licensing decisions.\n- Custom tasks (own datasets and metrics) run through the same flow.\n- Results are persisted and linked to the evaluated model's Model Registry version per canonical schema, including cost/duration metadata.\n- Rollback/compatibility: extending LMEvalJob must not break existing Tech Preview usage (backward-compatible CRD changes or documented migration).\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4, HLR-8.\n\n**BRANCH-B-E004 body:**\n\nTitle: New platform evaluation service (Outcome B)\n\nDescription: If the investigation recommends a new service, build the platform evaluation service wrapping lm-evaluation-harness, exposing the platform evaluation API (consumed by Dashboard, pipelines, and the Llama Stack eval provider), executing evaluations against platform-served models, and persisting results.\n\nScope:\n- New evaluation service (within the TrustyAI component's existing midstream repository unless the investigation calls for a standalone repo — see AC).\n- Evaluation execution against KServe endpoints and Llama Stack distributions.\n- Platform evaluation API surface for launching runs, checking status, retrieving results (consumed by