el Registry. No non-obvious mappings. All five have\narchitecture context files, so **all components are in the platform** — no\nonboarding chains (Rule 20) are triggered for the main plan.\n\nActive vs. passive classification:\n\n| Component | Class | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| TrustyAI | Active | Evaluation execution (LMEvalJob), result persistence writer |\n| Llama Stack | Active | Eval provider API must be backed by platform evaluation |\n| Dashboard | Active | Evaluation views, status, scores, comparison |\n| Data Science Pipelines | Active | Evaluation as embeddable pipeline step |\n| Model Registry | Passive | Custom properties on ModelVersion are the documented extension point; no code changes. Validated by a **different team** than the writer → separate validation Implementation epic (E005) per the passive-component exception |\n\n## Open Questions Triage (Step 3)\n\nAll three strategy open questions are genuine unknowns (explicitly flagged by\nthe strategy author) and each changes what downstream epics do:\n\n1. **Execution engine** (build on LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation service): the\n   answer changes which implementation epic exists and what it does. Two\n   bounded outcomes → Investigation **E001** with **conditional branch epics**\n   (`BRANCH-A-E004`, `BRANCH-B-E004`).\n2. **Result schema** (canonical format persisted to Model Registry custom\n   properties; survives suite version bumps): the schema is a build-against\n   artifact for persistence, Dashboard display, and the Llama Stack provider →\n   Investigation **E002** producing the committed schema. Gates downstream\n   epics that build against it (Rule 13).\n3. **Benchmark dataset licensing** (redistribute vs. customer fetch): the\n   answer changes what the out-of-the-box suite delivery in the execution epic\n   does → Investigation **E003** gating the execution epics' suite-packaging\n   scope.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|---