tes the suite-delivery scope (bundle vs. customer fetch) of the execution epics; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n161\t| E002 → E005 | 3, 13 | Validation is performed against the committed schema; if the schema fails version-bump resilience it is rewritten. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n162\t| E001 → E006 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the backend contract the Llama Stack provider delegates to; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n163\t| E002 → E006 | 13 | Provider result representation builds against the canonical schema. |\n164\t| E001 → E007 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the resource shapes/API the Dashboard reads (LMEvalJob CR vs. new service CR/API); true gate (`rewrite`). |\n165\t| E002 → E007 | 13 | Score display and comparison build against the persisted result schema. |\n166\t| E001 → E008 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the API the pipeline step calls; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n167\t| E002 → E008 | 13 | Score outputs exposed by the step derive from the result schema. |\n168\t| E005 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n169\t| E006 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n170\t| E007 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n171\t| E008 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n172\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E006/E007/E008 | 12, 13 + branch rule | Framework → consumer coupling: consumers build against the selected execution implementation. Because branch epics are not main-plan nodes, this coupling is enforced by acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008 (\"platform evaluation API/resource shapes available and documented\") rather than frontmatter dependencies — mirroring the AC-gate pattern of Rule 10/13 for references. |\n173\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E009 | 11 + branch rule | Docs describe the implemented capability; enforced via the \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC since branch epics are not main-plan nodes. |\n174\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | 14 | Independent investigations with independent inputs; all three start immediately. |\n175\t\n176\tRules checked and not applicable: 5-10, 20, 25 (no onboarding/Konflux epics —\n177\tevery touched repo is already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context);\n178\t19 (not safety-critical); 22 (no off-platform infrastructure).\n179\t\n180\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n181\t\n182\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n183\t|-----|----------|---------|\n184\t| HLR-1 Launch evaluations of served models | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n185\t| HLR-2 Standard suites out of the box | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (informed by E003) |\n186\t| HLR-3 Results persisted & linked to Model Registry version | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (write path); E005 (validation) |\n187\t| HLR-4 Custom evaluation tasks | P1 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n188\t| HLR-5 Dashboard runs, scores, comparison | P1 | E007 |\n189\t| HLR-6 Evaluation as pipeline step | P1 | E008 |\n190\t| HLR-7 Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform | P1 | E006 |\n191\t| HLR-8 Cost/duration metadata | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (schema fields defined in E002) |\n192\t| HLR-9 End-to-end product documentation | P2 | E009 |\n193\t\n194\tFull coverage — no orphaned HLRs. Note on HLR-4 and HLR-8: both are bundled\n195\tinto the execution epics rather than split out because they share the\n196\texecution path, run resource, and result schema with the P0 work (Step 4:\n197\ttightly coupled lower-priority scope stays bundled; splitting would duplicate\n198\tthe same data structures and API surface).\n199\t\n200\t## Conditional Branches\n201\t\n202\tE001 has two bounded outcomes; only one branch materializes:\n203\t\n204\t- **Outcome A — extend LMEvalJob** (`BRANCH-A-E004`): promote the Tech Preview\n205\t  LMEvalJob path to the supported single execution path. Gate failure impact:\n206\t  `remove` (this epic exists only if outcome A holds).\n207\t- **Outcome B — new evaluation service** (`BRANCH-B-E004`): build a new\n208\t  platform evaluation service wrapping lm-evaluation-harness. Gate failure\n209\t  impact: `remove`. If the investigation determines the service requires a\n210\t  standalone repository, the standard onboarding chain (repo-onboarding ∥\n211\t  license-validation → konflux-onboarding → implementation) prepends to this\n212\t  epic — the branch epic carries that as an explicit AC/contingency.\n213\t\n214\tBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document by the pipeline; they are\n215\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n216\t\n217\t## Health Warnings\n218\t\n219\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n220\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are absent (see parsing\n221\t  notes). Owner teams were inferred from architecture context — a human should\n222\t  confirm team assignments.\n223\t- **Docs epic priority**: E009 is P0 (docs-authoring exception: strategy\n224\t  priority `Critical` → P0) even though HLR-9 is P2. This is rule-compliant\n225\t  but reads as an inversion; flagged for human review.\n226\t- **Legal analysis on the P0 critical path**: E003 (licensing) gates the\n227\t  execution epics' out-of-the-box suite delivery. If legal turnaround is slow,\n228\t  a deferral option exists (ship the customer-fetch mechanism first and bundle\n229\t  allowlisted suites later) — noted here, not split into the DAG.\n230\t- **HLR-8 GPU-time metadata** depends on what the serving runtimes expose;\n231\t  partial availability is acceptable and captured as an AC on the execution\n232\t  epics rather than a separate epic.\n233\t- **Branch B repo contingency**: if the new service needs its own repository,\n234\t  the onboarding chain applies (noted on the branch epic; not pre-created\n235\t  since the outcome is undecided).\n236\t\n237\t## Tiered Delivery\n238\t\n239\tThe only externally-coupled work is E006 (Llama Stack eval provider), which\n240\tmay require an upstream contribution to `