{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1292b909-281d-495e-ae91-2d55a3010da8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-afee64de66d084e4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9002-6bjnt33f/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-9002-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-9002\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 10/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nThe decomposition is structurally sound in almost every respect — full HLR\ncoverage with a correct traceability matrix, clean component/team boundaries,\ncorrect Investigation/Implementation typing, well-justified conditional\nbranches, and consistent diagram/frontmatter dependencies. It fails on one\ncritical DAG defect: E009 (docs) consumes E003's licensing allowlist but has no\ndependency path to E003, so a data dependency discoverable from epic content is\nmissing from the DAG.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll nine HLRs map to epics in the traceability matrix, including the P2s\n(HLR-8 via schema fields in E002 plus capture in the branch execution epics;\nHLR-9 via E009). Priority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 are P0 as\ngates on P0 HLRs; E005 is P0 via HLR-3; E006/E007/E008 are P1 matching\nHLR-7/5/6. E009 is P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority\nCritical → P0), correctly applied and explicitly flagged for human review.\nHLR-4 (P1) and HLR-8 (P2) are bundled into the P0 branch execution epics with\nan explicit justification (shared execution path, run resource, and result\nschema); both are extensions of the same execution flow rather than\nindependently deferrable features, so the bundling is acceptable. Minor\nobservation only: HLR-4 (custom tasks) is the most deferrable of the bundled\nitems and could be argued into its own epic if independent deferral becomes a\nplanning requirement.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n\n**Critical issue — missing edge E003 → E009.** E009's scope states\n\"Disconnected-environment guidance reflecting the benchmark licensing\ndecisions: which suites ship in-product and which the customer must fetch,\"\nand its AC requires that guidance to be \"accurate per the benchmark licensing\nallowlist.\" That allowlist is E003's deliverable. E009's dependencies are\nE005, E006, E007, E008; their closures reach only E001 and E002. E003 is in\nno main-plan epic's transitive closure except the branch E004s (which\nthemselves are not main-plan dependencies of E009). The licensing outcome\nE009 depends on has no DAG representation — no edge, no AC gate ensuring E003\nis complete before E009's disconnected-environment content is written. Since\nE003 is a main-plan epic, a plain `dependencies` edge (E003 → E009) is\nexpressible and required. Per the review rules, a data/artifact dependency\ndiscoverable from epic content but absent from the DAG is a critical issue;\nthis criterion scores 0.\n\nEverything else in the DAG is coherent:\n\n- No circular dependencies; all referenced epics exist.\n- Diagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every solid edge in the mermaid\n  diagram matches a `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and\n  vice versa; arrows run dependency → dependent per convention.\n- Conditional branches are handled correctly: both `BRANCH-A-E004` and\n  `BRANCH-B-E004` set `branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and\n  `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in\n  `dependencies`; the branch-to-consumer couplings are enforced via\n  acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008/E009, which is the sanctioned pattern\n  given branch epics cannot appear in main-plan dependencies.\n- Parallelism is preserved: E001/E002/E003 run concurrently; E005/E006/E007/\n  E008 run concurrently after their gates; no unjustified serialization. No\n  konflux-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuous.\n- Minor: `critical_path_length: 3` reflects only the main-plan graph\n  (E002→E005→E009 or E001→E006→E009); the real execution chain including the\n  selected branch epic and its AC-gated consumers is depth 4-5, consistent\n  with an L-size strategy. The branch exclusion is documented, so this is a\n  reporting-convention note, not a defect.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEach epic maps to a single component/team tuple: TrustyAI (E001-E003, branch\nE004s), Model Registry (E005), RHOAI GenAI/Llama Stack (E006), RHOAI\nDashboard (E007), RHOAI Pipelines (E008), RHOAI Documentation (E009). The\npassive-component exception is correctly applied to Model Registry (E005 as a\nseparate validation epic owned by the consuming team). No cross-team bundling;\nno work-product type mixing (E008's \"documented example of a\npromotion-gating pipeline pattern\" is a usage example shipped with the\ncomponent, not standalone content authoring; E009 is pure docs). Minor\nobservations: (a) E002 and E005 both exercise a Model Registry custom\nproperties round-trip via the REST API — deliberate role separation\n(prototype vs. formal validation by the owning team), but the overlap could\nbe sharpened so E005 validates rather than repeats E002's work; (b)\nBRANCH-B-E004 (greenfield service: API surface, execution, suite delivery,\ncustom tasks, metadata, persistence, migration plan) is at the upper end of\nepic size, though it is one logical change on one team and splitting a\nconditional branch epic would multiply branch files.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n\nTyping is correct: E001's outcome selects which execution epic exists (true\nstructural gate, two bounded outcomes → conditional branches); E002 and E003\nresolve strategy-flagged open questions that change downstream scope; all\nImplementation epics produce concrete artifacts. E005 as Implementation (not\nInvestigation) is justified by the passive-component exception — it produces\na validation report artifact and does not change downstream structure. All\n`gated_by` targets appear in the corresponding epic's direct `dependencies`;\nall `gated_by` epics have `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and\n`fallback_approach` populated; no scheduling dependency is mislabeled as a\ngate.\n\nGating metadata issue: the DAG justification table labels the\nE003 → branch-E004 edges a \"true gate (`rewrite`)\" — the licensing outcome\nrewrites the branch epics' suite-delivery scope — yet `gated_by` on both\nbranch epics references only E001 (the schema is single-valued, and E001 is\nthe correct structural choice), and the E002/E003 co-dependencies of the\nbranch epics carry no gate representation beyond scope text and ACs. The\nstructural gate is properly set, so this does not cause incorrect execution,\nbut a gate the decomposition itself describes as \"true\" is not represented in\nthe gating metadata. Per the rubric this is a gating-metadata issue, not a\nmisclassification: score 1.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n\nSignal fields are type-correct: all three Investigation epics carry\n`investigation_signals` only; all Implementation epics (including both branch\nepics) carry `ai_signals` only. Rationale files exist for all ten epics and\nthe rationales match the frontmatter values. Investigation values are\nwell-argued (e.g., E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` for legal sign-off,\nE002 `local_runnability: 0` for the containerized registry server).\nBRANCH-B's greenfield scores (`change_specificity: 0`,\n`existing_foundation: 0`) versus BRANCH-A's extension scores are consistent\nwith the rubric.\n\nArguable values: E006, E007, and E008 all score `open_questions: -1` with the\nrationale that the backend contract \"is not fixed until the\nexecution-engine investigation resolves\" — but all three depend on (and are\ngated by) E001, the exact reasoning BRANCH-A-E004 uses to justify\n`open_questions: 0` (\"gating unknowns are resolved by the prerequisite\ninvestigations before this epic starts\"). The consumer epics' position is\ndefensible (they are only AC-gated, not dependency-gated, on the selected\nbranch implementation, so the actual API surface may not exist when they\nstart), but the same fact pattern is scored differently across sibling epics.\nPer the rubric (1-2 arguable values), score 1. Arithmetic/thresholds were not\nchecked, per instructions.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated\nACs are all present: BRANCH-A-E004 (replaces a Tech Preview capability) has\nthe backward-compatibility/migration AC; BRANCH-B-E004 (supersedes LMEvalJob)\nhas the migration/coexistence AC and the conditional onboarding AC including\n\"build pipeline is green\"; E009 (docs-authoring) has the\ntechnically-reviewed-against-implementation AC. E007's ACs encode the\nDashboard integration rules (CR-based, no private APIs, OdhDashboardConfig\nfeature flag). No epic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID — all\ncross-references use full epic IDs in frontmatter only, and body text refers\nto capabilities rather than IDs.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all HLRs (see\nCriterion 1), all three open questions (each becomes an Investigation with\ndownstream effect), the out-of-scope boundaries are respected (E008\nexplicitly keeps humans in promotion decisions; no arena-style evaluation or\nfine-tuning loops appear anywhere), and the conditional branches cover both\nbounded outcomes of the execution-engine question with explicit\ngate-failure impacts. Strategy-level context is carried forward: the\nTech-Preview-to-supported lifecycle concern appears in BRANCH-A's scope, the\nupstream-coupling risk for Llama Stack appears as E006's fallback AC and the\ntiered-delivery note, and the Branch-B standalone-repo contingency is\nrecorded on the branch epic. The disconnected-environment guidance content\nis covered in E009's scope — its DAG representation is the defect scored\nunder Criterion 2, not a scope gap.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":42802,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9e3011d0e9d9fb2","agent_id":"a8227aae6dade6acb","session_id":"959af684-2b7d-43b9-bdc0-4a564a1f5aaf","uuid":"d270f842-0287-4fb4-a7fa-b736af39821c","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:33:11.005Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
