{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dcc0f07c217f4f1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge E003 -> E009: E009 (docs) scope and ACs consume E003\n10\t    benchmark licensing allowlist (disconnected-environment guidance), but E009 dependencies\n11\t    (E005-E008) never reach E003 transitively. Add RHAISTRAT-9002-E003 to E009 dependencies\n12\t    (or an explicit AC gate) so the licensing outcome is available before the docs\n13\t    content is written.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n16\t  description: DAG justification labels E003 -> branch E004 edges a true gate (rewrite),\n17\t    but gated_by on both branch epics references only E001 (single-valued field).\n18\t    The licensing gate is represented only in scope text/ACs, not gating metadata.\n19\t- severity: minor\n20\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n21\t  description: open_questions scored -1 on E006/E007/E008 (backend contract not fixed\n22\t    until E001 resolves) but 0 on BRANCH-A-E004 for the same resolved-by-prerequisite-investigation\n23\t    reasoning; the consumer epic values are defensible (only AC-gated on the branch\n24\t    implementation) but inconsistent across siblings.\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n27\t  description: E002 and E005 both perform a Model Registry custom-properties round-trip\n28\t    via the REST API; deliberate role separation (prototype vs. owning-team validation)\n29\t    but the overlap could be sharpened so E005 validates rather than repeats E002\n30\t    work.\n31\t- severity: minor\n32\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n33\t  description: HLR-4 (P1 custom tasks) bundled into the P0 branch execution epics;\n34\t    justified as tightly coupled to the execution path, but it is the most independently\n35\t    deferrable bundled item if deferral planning is needed.\n36\terror: null\n37\t---\n38\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-9002\n39\t\n40\t## Review Summary\n41\t\n42\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n43\tRecommendation: revise\n44\t\n45\tThe decomposition is structurally sound in almost every respect — full HLR\n46\tcoverage with a correct traceability matrix, clean component/team boundaries,\n47\tcorrect Investigation/Implementation typing, well-justified conditional\n48\tbranches, and consistent diagram/frontmatter dependencies. It fails on one\n49\tcritical DAG defect: E009 (docs) consumes E003's licensing allowlist but has no\n50\tdependency path to E003, so a data dependency discoverable from epic content is\n51\tmissing from the DAG.\n52\t\n53\t## Criterion Details\n54\t\n55\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n56\t\n57\tAll nine HLRs map to epics in the traceability matrix, including the P2s\n58\t(HLR-8 via schema fields in E002 plus capture in the branch execution epics;\n59\tHLR-9 via E009). Priority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 are P0 as\n60\tgates on P0 HLRs; E005 is P0 via HLR-3; E006/E007/E008 are P1 matching\n61\tHLR-7/5/6. E009 is P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority\n62\tCritical → P0), correctly applied and explicitly flagged for human review.\n63\tHLR-4 (P1) and HLR-8 (P2) are bundled into the P0 branch execution epics with\n64\tan explicit justification (shared execution path, run resource, and result\n65\tschema); both are extensions of the same execution flow rather than\n66\tindependently deferrable features, so the bundling is acceptable. Minor\n67\tobservation only: HLR-4 (custom tasks) is the most deferrable of the bundled\n68\titems and could be argued into its own epic if independent deferral becomes a\n69\tplanning requirement.\n70\t\n71\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n72\t\n73\t**Critical issue — missing edge E003 → E009.** E009's scope states\n74\t\"Disconnected-environment guidance reflecting the benchmark licensing\n75\tdecisions: which suites ship in-product and which the customer must fetch,\"\n76\tand its AC requires that guidance to be \"accurate per the benchmark licensing\n77\tallowlist.\" That allowlist is E003's deliverable. E009's dependencies are\n78\tE005, E006, E007, E008; their closures reach only E001 and E002. E003 is in\n79\tno main-plan epic's transitive closure except the branch E004s (which\n80\tthemselves are not main-plan dependencies of E009). The licensing outcome\n81\tE009 depends on has no DAG representation — no edge, no AC gate ensuring E003\n82\tis complete before E009's disconnected-environment content is written. Since\n83\tE003 is a main-plan epic, a plain `dependencies` edge (E003 → E009) is\n84\texpressible and required. Per the review rules, a data/artifact dependency\n85\tdiscoverable from epic content but absent from the DAG is a critical issue;\n86\tthis criterion scores 0.\n87\t\n88\tEverything else in the DAG is coherent:\n89\t\n90\t- No circular dependencies; all referenced epics exist.\n91\t- Diagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every solid edge in the mermaid\n92\t  diagram matches a `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and\n93\t  vice versa; arrows run dependency → dependent per convention.\n94\t- Conditional branches are handled correctly: both `BRANCH-A-E004` and\n95\t  `BRANCH-B-E004` set `branch` (A/B, matching filenames) and\n96\t  `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in\n97\t  `dependencies`; the branch-to-consumer couplings are enforced via\n98\t  acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008/E009, which is the sanctioned pattern\n99\t  given branch epics cannot appear in main-plan dependencies.\n100\t- Parallelism is preserved: E001/E002/E003 run concurrently; E005/E006/E007/\n101\t  E008 run concurrently after their gates; no unjustified serialization. No\n102\t  konflux-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 pairings are vacuous.\n103\t- Minor: `critical_path_length: 3` reflects only the main-plan graph\n104\t  (E002→E005→E009 or E001→E006→E009); the real execution chain including the\n105\t  selected branch epic and its AC-gated consumers is depth 4-5, consistent\n106\t  with an L-size strategy. The branch exclusion is documented, so this is a\n107\t  reporting-convention note, not a defect.\n108\t\n109\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n110\t\n111\tEach epic maps to a single component/team tuple: TrustyAI (E001-E003, branch\n112\tE004s), Model Registry (E005), RHOAI GenAI/Llama Stack (E006), RHOAI\n113\tDashboard (E007), RHOAI Pipelines (E008), RHOAI Documentation (E009). The\n114\tpassive-component exception is correctly applied to Model Registry (E005 as a\n115\tseparate validation epic owned by the consuming team). No cross-team bundling;\n116\tno work-product type mixing (E008's \"documented example of a\n117\tpromotion-gating pipeline pattern\" is a usage example shipped with the\n118\tcomponent, not standalone content authoring; E009 is pure docs). Minor\n119\tobservations: (a) E002 and E005 both exercise a Model Registry custom\n120\tproperties round-trip via the REST API — deliberate role separation\n121\t(prototype vs. formal validation by the owning team), but the overlap could\n122\tbe sharpened so E005 validates rather than repeats E002's work; (b)\n123\tBRANCH-B-E004 (greenfield service: API surface, execution, suite delivery,\n124\tcustom tasks, metadata, persistence, migration plan) is at the upper end of\n125\tepic size, though it is one logical change on one team and splitting a\n126\tconditional branch epic would multiply branch files.\n127\t\n128\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n129\t\n130\tTyping is correct: E001's outcome selects which execution epic exists (true\n131\tstructural gate, two bounded outcomes → conditional branches); E002 and E003\n132\tresolve strategy-flagged open questions that change downstream scope; all\n133\tImplementation epics produce concrete artifacts. E005 as Implementation (not\n134\tInvestigation) is justified by the passive-component exception — it produces\n135\ta validation report artifact and does not change downstream structure. All\n136\t`gated_by` targets appear in the corresponding epic's direct `dependencies`;\n137\tall `gated_by` epics have `gate_failure_impact` with both `action` and\n138\t`fallback_approach` populated; no scheduling dependency is mislabeled as a\n139\tgate.\n140\t\n141\tGating metadata issue: the DAG justification table labels the\n142\tE003 → branch-E004 edges a \"true gate (`rewrite`)\" — the licensing outcome\n143\trewrites the branch epics' suite-delivery scope — yet `gated_by` on both\n144\tbranch epics references only E001 (the schema is single-valued, and E001 is\n145\tthe correct structural choice), and the E002/E003 co-dependencies of the\n146\tbranch epics carry no gate representation beyond scope text and ACs. The\n147\tstructural gate is properly set, so this does not cause incorrect execution,\n148\tbut a gate the decomposition itself describes as \"true\" is not represented in\n149\tthe gating metadata. Per the rubric this is a gating-metadata issue, not a\n150\tmisclassification: score 1.\n151\t\n152\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n153\t\n154\tSignal fields are type-correct: all three Investigation epics carry\n155\t`investigation_signals` only; all Implementation epics (including both branch\n156\tepics) carry `ai_signals` only. Rationale files exist for all ten epics and\n157\tthe rationales match the frontmatter values. Investigation values are\n158\twell-argued (e.g., E003 `human_judgment_required: -1` for legal sign-off,\n159\tE002 `local_runnability: 0` for the containerized registry server).\n160\tBRANCH-B's greenfield scores (`change_specificity: 0`,\n161\t`existing_foundation: 0`) versus BRANCH-A's extension scores are consistent\n162\twith the rubric.\n163\t\n164\tArguable values: E006, E007, and E008 all score `open_questions: -1` with the\n165\trationale that the backend contract \"is not fixed until the\n166\texecution-engine investigation resolves\" — but all three depend on (and are\n167\tgated by) E001, the exact reasoning BRANCH-A-E004 uses to justify\n168\t`open_questions: 0` (\"gating unknowns are resolved by the prerequisite\n169\tinvestigations before this epic starts\"). The consumer epics' position is\n170\tdefensible (they are only AC-gated, not dependency-gated, on the selected\n171\tbranch implementation, so the actual API surface may not exist when they\n172\tstart), but the same fact pattern is scored differently across sibling epics.\n173\tPer the rubric (1-2 arguable values), score 1. Arithmetic/thresholds were not\n174\tchecked, per instructions.\n175\t\n176\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n177\t\n178\tEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated\n179\tACs are all present: BRANCH-A-E004 (replaces a Tech Preview capability) has\n180\tthe backward-compatibility/migration AC; BRANCH-B-E004 (supersedes LMEvalJob)\n181\thas the migration/coexistence AC and the conditional onboarding AC including\n182\t\"build pipeline is green\"; E009 (docs-authoring) has the\n183\ttechnically-reviewed-against-implementation AC. E007's ACs encode the\n184\tDashboard integration rules (CR-based, no private APIs, OdhDashboardConfig\n185\tfeature flag). No epic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID — all\n186\tcross-references use full epic IDs in frontmatter only, and body text refers\n187\tto capabilities rather than IDs.\n188\t\n189\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n190\t\n191\tThe combined epic set accounts for the full strategy scope: all HLRs (see\n192\tCriterion 1), all three open questions (each becomes an Investigation with\n193\tdownstream effect), the out-of-scope boundaries are respected (E008\n194\texplicitly keeps humans in promotion decisions; no arena-style evaluation or\n195\tfine-tuning loops appear anywhere), and the conditional branches cover both\n196\tbounded outcomes of the execution-engine question with explicit\n197\tgate-failure impacts. Strategy-level context is carried forward: the\n198\tTech-Preview-to-supported lifecycle concern appears in BRANCH-A's scope, the\n199\tupstream-coupling risk for Llama Stack appears as E006's fallback AC and the\n200\ttiered-delivery note, and the Branch-B standalone-repo contingency is\n201\trecorded on the branch epic. The disconnected-environment guidance content\n202\tis covered in E009's scope — its DAG representation is the defect scored\n203\tunder Criterion 2, not a scope gap.\n204\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c161d6855481173","agent_id":"a4ff73ddc2cc9d9c6","session_id":"959af684-2b7d-43b9-bdc0-4a564a1f5aaf","uuid":"c66da755-b194-47f4-8095-9a9cfa31ab0d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:33:46.922Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dcc0f07c217f4f1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
