ctly, branch\n39\tfiles are correctly formed, signal scoring is type-appropriate and justified,\n40\tand all rule-mandated acceptance criteria are present.\n41\t\n42\t## Criterion Details\n43\t\n44\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n45\tAll nine HLRs map to at least one epic, and every epic's HLR Traceability\n46\tsection is consistent with the summary matrix. P0 HLRs (HLR-1/2/3) are covered\n47\tby the conditional branch execution epics plus E005 (validation side of\n48\tHLR-3). P1 HLRs map to E007 (HLR-5), E008 (HLR-6), E006 (HLR-7). P2 HLRs are\n49\tcovered, not silently dropped: HLR-8 fields are defined in E002's schema and\n50\trecorded by the execution epics; HLR-9 is E009.\n51\t\n52\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001/E002/E003 are P0 (each gates P0 HLRs),\n53\tE005 is P0 (validates P0 HLR-3), E006/E007/E008 are P1 (they enable only P1\n54\tHLRs), and E009 is P0 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter\n55\t`priority: Critical` → P0), which is correctly not treated as a priority\n56\tinheritance violation.\n57\t\n58\tPriority collapse was examined: the branch execution epics bundle P1 HLR-4\n59\t(custom evaluation tasks) and P2 HLR-8 (cost/duration metadata) with the P0\n60\twork. This is acceptable under the Step 4 coupling test — HLR-4 runs through\n61\tthe same execution flow, run resource, and result schema as the P0 work (it\n62\tshares the API surface and data structures, so splitting would force two epics\n63\tto build the same execution path independently), and HLR-8's metadata fields\n64\tare interwoven with the canonical result schema (incidental to the P0 write\n65\tpath). The decomposition also documents the deferral carve-out path for HLR-4\n66\texplicitly. No deferrable-feature priority collapse.\n67\t\n68\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n69\tCross-checked every diagram edge against epic frontmatter `dependencies` and\n70\tvice versa: exact match in both directions, arrows drawn dependency →\n71\tdependent per the convention. No circular dependencies. No main-plan epic\n72\tlists a branch epic in its `dependencies`. Both branch files\n73\t(`BRANCH-A-E004`, `BRANCH-B-E004`) set `branch` (A/B, matching filename) and\n74\t`gated_by: E001`, with `gate_failure_impact` populated — the pipeline will not\n75\tsilently drop them. No konflux-onboarding or repo-onboarding epics exist, so\n76\tthe Rule 7/8/10 parallelism checks are vacuously satisfied; the Branch B\n77\tonboarding contingency is correctly carried as an AC, not a DAG edge. The\n78\tE002/E003 investigation couplings on the branch epics and E006–E008 are\n79\thandled via the documented AC-gate pattern, consistent with the single-valued\n80\t`gated_by` field holding the structural gate (E001). E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 run in\n81\tparallel; E005–E008 run in parallel; naturally parallel work is not\n82\tserialized. Critical path 3 is reasonable for this strategy: it is wide (five\n83\tcomponents) rather than deep, and branch-consumer coupling is enforced via AC\n84\tgates because branch epics cannot appear in main-plan dependencies. The\n85\tE003 → E009 edge is justified as a real data dependency (docs' disconnected-\n86\tenvironment guidance consumes the licensing allowlist). Note the gate metadata\n87\tdefect on that edge is recorded under Criterion 4.\n88\t\n89\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n90\tComponent/team tuples are clean: each epic is owned by one team (TrustyAI,\n91\tModel Registry, RHOAI GenAI, RHOAI Dashboard, RHOAI Pipelines, RHOAI\n92\tDocumentation) and touches one component. The three TrustyAI Investigations\n93\tare separate unknowns, which is permitted (boundary rule applies to\n94\tImplementations). The E002 (writer-side proof of concept) vs E005\n95\t(owning-team formal validation) split is not scope duplication — it is the\n96\tpassive-component exception applied correctly, and both epics explicitly\n97\tstate they do not repeat each other's work. No epic bundles multiple teams.\n98\tTwo minor observations (below minor threshold, no score impact):\n99\t- E008 bundles a documented example of a promotion-gating pipeline pattern\n100\t  (content authoring) with the KFP component code — borderline work-product\n101\t  mixing, though a single usage example intrinsic to the component deliverable\n102\t  is closer to incidental documentation than a separate content work stream.\n103\t- The branch execution epics are chunky (targeting, suite delivery, custom\n104\t  tasks, metadata, persistence, plus preview-to-supported migration), and\n105\t  could be split within each branch if scheduling pressure demands it.\n106\t\n107\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n108\tTypes are correctly assigned — E001/E002/E003 genuinely resolve uncertainty\n109\tthat changes downstream structure (engine selection has two bounded outcomes\n110\twith branch epics; the schema and licensing outcomes rewrite downstream\n111\tscope), and every Implementation epic produces a concrete artifact. However,\n112\tthere is one Major gating-metadata defect:\n113\t\n114\t- **E009 lists Investigation E003 in its `dependencies` but has\n115\t  `gated_by: null`.** Per Rule 3, an Investigation that determines the scope\n116\t  of downstream work must gate it, and the review rubric requires every epic\n117\t  that lists an Investigation in `dependencies` to have `gated_by` set. The\n118\t  licensing outcome rewrites the disconnected-environment guidance content\n119\t  that E009 must produce (the decomposition's own DAG justification says the\n120\t  docs epic \"cannot write accurate licensing guidance without its artifact\"),\n121\t  so E009 should record `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E003` with\n122\t  `gate_failure_impact` (action `rewrite`, fallback: guidance rewritten\n123\t  against the final per-suite delivery decisions). Every other gated epic\n124\t  (both branch epics, E005, E006, E007, E008) records its gate correctly,\n125\t  which makes the omission on E009 a real inconsistency automated consumers\n126\t  will miss.\n127\t\n128\tAll `gated_by` targets appear in the corresponding epics' direct\n129\t`dependencies` lists, and every non-null `gated_by` has both\n130\t`gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` populated.\n131\t\n132\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n133\tSignal sets are type-appropriate: all three Investigations carry\n134\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); all Implementation epics (including\n135\tthe branch epics and the docs-authoring epic) carry `ai_signals` (9-signal\n136\trubric). No epic mixes sets. Every signal rationale file exists, matches the\n137\tfrontmatter values, and is justified against the strategy content. Spot\n138\tchecks: E003 `source_accessibility: 1` is defensible (license terms are\n139\tpublic records that contain the answer; the legal sign-off is correctly\n140\tcaptured under `human_judgment_required: -1` instead); E001\n141\t`cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` correctly limits the cluster dependence to\n142\tperipheral behavioral questions; Branch B's `change_specificity: 0` /\n143\t`existing_foundation: 0` correctly reflect greenfield service work while\n144\tBranch A scores 1/1 for extending the existing CRD; E006's\n145\t`external_dependency: -1` matches the upstream `meta-llama/llama-stack`\n146\tcontribution risk with the documented midstream carry fallback. The\n147\tE006/E007/E008 `open_questions: -1` rationales coherently distinguish the\n148\tresidual API-shape uncertainty (coupled only via AC gate) from the resolved\n149\tengine choice. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed, per\n150\tinstructions.\n151\t\n152\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n153\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy.\n154\tRule-mandated ACs are present on every epic that meets the rule's criteria:\n155\tBranch A carries the backward-compatibility/migration AC for replacing Tech\n156\tPreview LMEvalJob usage; Branch B carries a migration/coexistence plan AC for\n157\tsuperseding the LMEvalJob path plus the build-pipeline-green AC for its\n158\tonboarding contingency; E009 (docs-authoring) carries the\n159\t\"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC. No epic body references\n160\tsibling epics by draft ID — dependencies are described in plain terms\n161\tthroughout.\n162\t\n163\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n164\tFull scope coverage: every HLR, the problem-statement capability (launch,\n165\tpersist, compare), and all three strategy open questions are accounted for\n166\t(each open question became a genuine Investigation with downstream gates).\n167\tOut-of-scope boundaries are respected (no arena-style evaluation, no\n168\tautomated promotion — E008 explicitly keeps humans in the promotion decision,\n169\tno fine-tuning feedback loops). The conditional branches cover both bounded\n170\toutcomes of the execution-engine question (A: extend LMEvalJob; B: new\n171\tservice, including the standalone-repo onboarding contingency). Cross-epic\n172\tconsistency holds: E002's schema items (scores, suite/task/version identity,\n173\tcost/duration metadata) are consumed by the branch epics, E005, E006, E007,\n174\tand E008; the licensing allowlist flows to the branch epics (AC gate) and\n175\tE009 (data dependency). Strategy-level context (open questions, external\n176\tupstream coupling with the midstream-carry fallback, GPU-time metadata\n177\tcaveat) is carried forward into the relevant epics and health warnings, and\n178\tthe docs requirement (HLR-9) has its docs-authoring epic.\n179\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89e02b7c9e6c5f86","agent_id":"a7241ea686a24573b","session_id":"959af684-2b7d-43b9-bdc0-4a564a1f5aaf","uuid":"560dc542-bcc7-4318-a992-1abb8c4a05d0","timestamp":"2026-10-21T23:54:07.931Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae59e66f5f44ff3e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-revise RHAISTRAT-9002 decomposition"}
