pics. |\n174\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E006/E007/E008 | 12, 13 + branch rule | Framework → consumer coupling: consumers build against the selected execution implementation. Because branch epics are not main-plan nodes, this coupling is enforced by acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008 (\"platform evaluation API/resource shapes available and documented\") rather than frontmatter dependencies — mirroring the AC-gate pattern of Rule 10/13 for references. |\n175\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E009 | 11 + branch rule | Docs describe the implemented capability; enforced via the \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC since branch epics are not main-plan nodes. |\n176\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | 14 | Independent investigations with independent inputs; all three start immediately. |\n177\t\n178\tRules checked and not applicable: 5-10, 20, 25 (no onboarding/Konflux epics —\n179\tevery touched repo is already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context);\n180\t19 (not safety-critical); 22 (no off-platform infrastructure).\n181\t\n182\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n183\t\n184\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n185\t|-----|----------|---------|\n186\t| HLR-1 Launch evaluations of served models | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n187\t| HLR-2 Standard suites out of the box | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (informed by E003) |\n188\t| HLR-3 Results persisted & linked to Model Registry version | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (write path); E005 (validation) |\n189\t| HLR-4 Custom evaluation tasks | P1 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n190\t| HLR-5 Dashboard runs, scores, comparison | P1 | E007 |\n191\t| HLR-6 Evaluation as pipeline step | P1 | E008 |\n192\t| HLR-7 Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform | P1 | E006 |\n193\t| HLR-8 Cost/duration metadata | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (schema fields defined in E002) |\n194\t| HLR-9 End-to-end product documentation | P2 | E009 |\n195\t\n196\tFull coverage — no orphaned HLRs. Note on HLR-4 and HLR-8: both are bundled\n197\tinto the execution epics rather than split out because they share the\n198\texecution path, run resource, and result schema with the P0 work (Step 4:\n199\ttightly coupled lower-priority scope stays bundled; splitting would duplicate\n200\tthe same data structures and API surface). Deferral note: if independent\n201\tdeferral planning becomes necessary, HLR-4 (custom evaluation tasks) is the\n202\tmost independently deferrable of the bundled items — it is an additive\n203\tcapability on the same execution flow and could be carved into its own epic\n204\twithout disturbing the P0 path, whereas HLR-8's metadata fields are\n205\tinterwoven with the canonical result schema and are less separable.\n206\t\n207\t## Conditional Branches\n208\t\n209\tE001 has two bounded outcomes; only one branch materializes:\n210\t\n211\t- **Outcome A — extend LMEvalJob** (`BRANCH-A-E004`): promote the Tech Preview\n212\t  LMEvalJob path to the supported single execution path. Gate failure impact:\n213\t  `remove` (this epic exists only if outcome A holds).\n214\t- **Outcome B — new evaluation service** (`BRANCH-B-E004`): build a new\n215\t  platform evaluation service wrapping lm-evaluation-harness. Gate failure\n216\t  impact: `remove`. If the investigation determines the service requires a\n217\t  standalone repository, the standard onboarding chain (repo-onboarding ∥\n218\t  license-validation → konflux-onboarding → implementation) prepends to this\n219\t  epic — the branch epic carries that as an explicit AC/contingency.\n220\t\n221\tBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document by the pipeline; they are\n222\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n223\t\n224\t## Health Warnings\n225\t\n226\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n227\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are absent (see parsing\n228\t  notes). Owner teams were inferred from architecture context — a human should\n229\t  confirm team assignments.\n230\t- **Docs epic priority**: E009 is P0 (docs-authoring exception: strategy\n231\t  priority `Critical` → P0) even though HLR-9 is P2. This is rule-compliant\n232\t  but reads as an inversion; flagged for human review.\n233\t- **Legal analysis on the P0 critical path**: E003 (licensing) gates the\n234\t  execution epics' out-of-the-box suite delivery. If legal turnaround is slow,\n235\t  a deferral option exists (ship the customer-fetch mechanism first and bundle\n236\t  allowlisted suites later) — noted here, not split into the DAG.\n237\t- **HLR-8 GPU-time metadata** depends on what the serving runtimes expose;\n238\t  partial availability is acceptable and captured as an AC on the execution\n239\t  epics rather than a separate epic.\n240\t- **Branch B repo contingency**: if the new service needs its own repository,\n241\t  the onboarding chain applies (noted on the branch epic; not pre-created\n242\t  since the outcome is undecided).\n243\t\n244\t## Tiered Delivery\n245\t\n246\tThe only externally-coupled work is E006 (Llama Stack eval provider), which\n247\tmay require an upstream contribution to `meta-llama/llama-stack` with\n248\tuncertain acceptance timing. Per Rule 21, a viable fallback exists (midstream\n249\tcarry / cherry-pick in `opendatahub-io/llama-stack-k8s-operator`), so it is\n250\trecorded as an AC on E006, not a separate epic:\n251\t\n252\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: core evaluation capability — execution\n253\t  (branch epic), persistence validation (E005), Dashboard views (E007),\n254\t  pipeline step (E008).\n255\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E006 — Llama Stack provider, P1, blocks no\n256\t  other epic.\n257\t\n258\tNo other external dependencies have uncertain timing; Model Registry,\n259\tTrustyAI, Dashboard, and Data Science Pipelines are all in-platform with\n260\tKonflux-onboarded midstream repos.\n261\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b75db364a0cdb1b7","agent_id":"ac52824ac12a50737","session_id":"959af684-2b7d-