writes the suite-delivery scope (bundle vs. customer fetch) of the execution epics; true gate (`rewrite`). The single-valued `gated_by` field holds E001 (the structural gate), so the licensing rewrite gate is represented as an explicit acceptance-criteria gate on both branch epics: suite-delivery scope must be finalized against the benchmark licensing allowlist before the epic is done. |\n161\t| E002 → E005 | 3, 13 | Validation is performed against the committed schema; if the schema fails version-bump resilience it is rewritten. True gate (`rewrite`). |\n162\t| E001 → E006 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the backend contract the Llama Stack provider delegates to; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n163\t| E002 → E006 | 13 | Provider result representation builds against the canonical schema. |\n164\t| E001 → E007 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the resource shapes/API the Dashboard reads (LMEvalJob CR vs. new service CR/API); true gate (`rewrite`). |\n165\t| E002 → E007 | 13 | Score display and comparison build against the persisted result schema. |\n166\t| E001 → E008 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the API the pipeline step calls; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n167\t| E002 → E008 | 13 | Score outputs exposed by the step derive from the result schema. |\n168\t| E003 → E009 | 3, 11 | True gate: the docs' disconnected-environment guidance consumes the benchmark licensing allowlist (which suites ship in-product vs. customer fetch), so the licensing outcome rewrites that content. Recorded on E009 as `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E003` with `gate_failure_impact` action `rewrite` (fallback: guidance rewritten against the final per-suite delivery decisions). |\n169\t| E005 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n170\t| E006 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n171\t| E007 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n172\t| E008 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n173\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E006/E007/E008 | 12, 13 + branch rule | Framework → consumer coupling: consumers build against the selected execution implementation. Because branch epics are not main-plan nodes, this coupling is enforced by acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008 (\"platform evaluation API/resource shapes available and documented\") rather than frontmatter dependencies — mirroring the AC-gate pattern of Rule 10/13 for references. |\n174\t| (dashed) branch E004 → E009 | 11 + branch rule | Docs describe the implemented capability; enforced via the \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC since branch epics are not main-plan nodes. |\n175\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | 14 | Independent investigations with independent inputs; all three start immediately. |\n176\t\n177\tRules checked and not applicable: 5-10, 20, 25 (no onboarding/Konflux epics —\n178\tevery touched repo is already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context);\n179\t19 (not safety-critical); 22 (no off-platform infrastructure).\n180\t\n181\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n182\t\n183\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n184\t|-----|----------|---------|\n185\t| HLR-1 Launch evaluations of served models | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n186\t| HLR-2 Standard suites out of the box | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (informed by E003) |\n187\t| HLR-3 Results persisted & linked to Model Registry version | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (write path); E005 (validation) |\n188\t| HLR-4 Custom evaluation tasks | P1 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n189\t| HLR-5 Dashboard runs, scores, comparison | P1 | E007 |\n190\t| HLR-6 Evaluation as pipeline step | P1 | E008 |\n191\t| HLR-7 Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform | P1 | E006 |\n192\t| HLR-8 Cost/duration metadata | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (schema fields defined in E002) |\n193\t| HLR-9 End-to-end product documentation | P2 | E009 |\n194\t\n195\tFull coverage — no orphaned HLRs. Note on HLR-4 and HLR-8: both are bundled\n196\tinto the execution epics rather than split out because they share the\n197\texecution path, run resource, and result schema with the P0 work (Step 4:\n198\ttightly coupled lower-priority scope stays bundled; splitting would duplicate\n199\tthe same data structures and API surface). Deferral note: if independent\n200\tdeferral planning becomes necessary, HLR-4 (custom evaluation tasks) is the\n201\tmost independently deferrable of the bundled items — it is an additive\n202\tcapability on the same execution flow and could be carved into its own epic\n203\twithout disturbing the P0 path, whereas HLR-8's metadata fields are\n204\tinterwoven with the canonical result schema and are less separable.\n205\t\n206\t## Conditional Branches\n207\t\n208\tE001 has two bounded outcomes; only one branch materializes:\n209\t\n210\t- **Outcome A — extend LMEvalJob** (`BRANCH-A-E004`): promote the Tech Preview\n211\t  LMEvalJob path to the supported single execution path. Gate failure impact:\n212\t  `remove` (this epic exists only if outcome A holds).\n213\t- **Outcome B — new evaluation service** (`BRANCH-B-E004`): build a new\n214\t  platform evaluation service wrapping lm-evaluation-harness. Gate failure\n215\t  impact: `remove`. If the investigation determines the service requires a\n216\t  standalone repository, the standard onboarding chain (repo-onboarding ∥\n217\t  license-validation → konflux-onboarding → implementation) prepends to this\n218\t  epic — the branch epic carries that as an explicit AC/contingency.\n219\t\n220\tBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document by the pipeline; they are\n221\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n222\t\n223\t## Health Warnings\n224\t\n225\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n226\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are absent (see parsing\n227\t  notes). Owner teams were inferred from architecture context — a human should\n228\t  confirm team assignments.\n229\t- **Docs