{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c8073be58ee0c65","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9002\n3\tepic_count: 14\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9002 — Integrated GenAI model evaluation for RHOAI\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below threshold: the strategy spans five components and at least five teams\n15\t(TrustyAI, RHOAI GenAI, RHOAI Dashboard, RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry), with\n16\tHLRs from P0 to P2 and multiple architecturally distinct work streams\n17\t(execution engine, UI, pipeline integration, provider API, docs). Not\n18\tdocs-only: substantial code changes across active components. Full\n19\tdecomposition performed (Steps 1-8).\n20\t\n21\t## Strategy Parsing Notes (Step 1)\n22\t\n23\tSections present: Problem Statement, High-Level Requirements (with P0/P1/P2),\n24\tAffected Components (bare list), Out of Scope, Open Questions.\n25\t\n26\tSections missing (health warnings, decomposition proceeded with available\n27\tinformation):\n28\t\n29\t- **Affected Components is a bare list** — no per-component change description\n30\t  or owning team. Owner teams were inferred from the architecture context\n31\t  files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`).\n32\t- **No Impacted Teams table** — teams inferred as above.\n33\t- **No Dependencies table** — external coupling inferred from architecture\n34\t  context (Llama Stack upstream `meta-llama/llama-stack`).\n35\t- **No Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria** — epic ACs derived from HLRs and\n36\t  the problem statement.\n37\t- **No Non-Functional Requirements.**\n38\t- **No Staff Engineer Input section** (Step 1.5 skipped — nothing to override).\n39\t\n40\t### Component mapping (Step 2)\n41\t\n42\tAll five strategy components match canonical names in\n43\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` exactly: TrustyAI, Llama Stack, Dashboard,\n44\tData Science Pipelines, Model Registry. No non-obvious mappings. All five have\n45\tarchitecture context files, so **all components are in the platform** — no\n46\tonboarding chains (Rule 20) are triggered for the main plan.\n47\t\n48\tActive vs. passive classification:\n49\t\n50\t| Component | Class | Rationale |\n51\t|-----------|-------|-----------|\n52\t| TrustyAI | Active | Evaluation execution (LMEvalJob), result persistence writer |\n53\t| Llama Stack | Active | Eval provider API must be backed by platform evaluation |\n54\t| Dashboard | Active | Evaluation views, status, scores, comparison |\n55\t| Data Science Pipelines | Active | Evaluation as embeddable pipeline step |\n56\t| Model Registry | Passive | Custom properties on ModelVersion are the documented extension point; no code changes. Validated by a **different team** than the writer → separate validation Implementation epic (E005) per the passive-component exception |\n57\t\n58\t## Open Questions Triage (Step 3)\n59\t\n60\tAll three strategy open questions are genuine unknowns (explicitly flagged by\n61\tthe strategy author) and each changes what downstream epics do:\n62\t\n63\t1. **Execution engine** (build on LMEvalJob vs. new evaluation service): the\n64\t   answer changes which implementation epic exists and what it does. Two\n65\t   bounded outcomes → Investigation **E001** with **conditional branch epics**\n66\t   — per outcome, an execution epic (`BRANCH-A-E004` / `BRANCH-B-E004`), a\n67\t   result-persistence epic (`BRANCH-A-E010` / `BRANCH-B-E012`), and a\n68\t   custom-task epic (`BRANCH-A-E011` / `BRANCH-B-E013`).\n69\t2. **Result schema** (canonical format persisted to Model Registry custom\n70\t   properties; survives suite version bumps): the schema is a build-against\n71\t   artifact for persistence, Dashboard display, and the Llama Stack provider →\n72\t   Investigation **E002** producing the committed schema. Gates downstream\n73\t   epics that build against it (Rule 13).\n74\t3. **Benchmark dataset licensing** (redistribute vs. customer fetch): the\n75\t   answer changes what the out-of-the-box suite delivery in the execution epic\n76\t   does → Investigation **E003** gating the execution epics' suite-packaging\n77\t   scope.\n78\t\n79\t## Epic List\n80\t\n81\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n82\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n83\t| E001 | Evaluation execution engine investigation | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n84\t| E002 | Canonical evaluation result schema prototype | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n85\t| E003 | Benchmark dataset licensing and redistribution analysis | Investigation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n86\t| BRANCH-A-E004 | Extend LMEvalJob into the supported evaluation execution path | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n87\t| BRANCH-A-E010 | Persist evaluation results to Model Registry (Outcome A) | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n88\t| BRANCH-A-E011 | Custom evaluation task support (Outcome A) | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n89\t| BRANCH-B-E004 | New platform evaluation service | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n90\t| BRANCH-B-E012 | Persist evaluation results to Model Registry (Outcome B) | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P0 |\n91\t| BRANCH-B-E013 | Custom evaluation task support (Outcome B) | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n92\t| E005 | Validate evaluation result persistence in Model Registry | Implementation | Model Registry | Model Registry | P0 |\n93\t| E006 | Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform evaluation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P1 |\n94\t| E007 | Dashboard evaluation views | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n95\t| E008 | Evaluation step for Data Science Pipelines | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | P1 |\n96\t| E009 | Evaluation workflow product documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | Documentation | P0 |\n97\t\n98\tPriority notes:\n99\t\n100\t- E001/E002/E003 are P0 by priority inheritance: each gates P0 HLRs\n101\t  (HLR-1/2/3 for E001 and E002; HLR-2's out-of-the-box delivery for E003).\n102\t- E005 is P0 by inheritance from HLR-3 (P0), which it validates.\n103\t- **E009 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception** (strategy\n104\t  frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), *not* priority inheritance — see\n105\t  Health Warnings.\n106\t- Branch execution epics (BRANCH-*-E004) and branch persistence epics\n107\t  (BRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E012) are P0 (HLR-1/2/3).\n108\t- Branch custom-task epics (BRANCH-A-E011 / BRANCH-B-E013) are P1, matching\n109\t  HLR-4's priority — the carve-out exists precisely so this deferrable\n110\t  feature is planned independently of the P0 path.\n111\t\n112\t## Dependency DAG\n113\t\n114\tBranch epics are conditional alternatives, not main-plan\n115\tnodes; only one branch set — `BRANCH-A-E004`/`BRANCH-A-E010`/`BRANCH-A-E011`\n116\tor `BRANCH-B-E004`/`BRANCH-B-E012`/`BRANCH-B-E013` — will materialize\n117\tdepending on E001's outcome. Solid edges are frontmatter `dependencies`\n118\t(within-branch edges included); dashed edges are build-against couplings\n119\tenforced via acceptance criteria because branch epics cannot appear in\n120\tmain-plan dependencies.\n121\t\n122\t```mermaid\n123\tgraph TD\n124\t    E001[E001 Execution engine investigation]\n125\t    E002[E002 Result schema investigation]\n126\t    E003[E003 Benchmark licensing investigation]\n127\t    BA[BRANCH-A-E004 Extend LMEvalJob]\n128\t    BA10[BRANCH-A-E010 Persist results A]\n129\t    BA11[BRANCH-A-E011 Custom tasks A]\n130\t    BB[BRANCH-B-E004 New evaluation service]\n131\t    BB12[BRANCH-B-E012 Persist results B]\n132\t    BB13[BRANCH-B-E013 Custom tasks B]\n133\t    E005[E005 Model Registry validation]\n134\t    E006[E006 Llama Stack eval provider]\n135\t    E007[E007 Dashboard evaluation views]\n136\t    E008[E008 Evaluation pipeline step]\n137\t    E009[E009 Evaluation docs]\n138\t\n139\t    E001 --> BA\n140\t    E002 --> BA\n141\t    E003 --> BA\n142\t    E001 --> BA10\n143\t    E002 --> BA10\n144\t    BA --> BA10\n145\t    E001 --> BA11\n146\t    BA --> BA11\n147\t\n148\t    E001 --> BB\n149\t    E002 --> BB\n150\t    E003 --> BB\n151\t    E001 --> BB12\n152\t    E002 --> BB12\n153\t    BB --> BB12\n154\t    E001 --> BB13\n155\t    BB --> BB13\n156\t\n157\t    E002 --> E005\n158\t\n159\t    E001 --> E006\n160\t    E002 --> E006\n161\t    E001 --> E007\n162\t    E002 --> E007\n163\t    E001 --> E008\n164\t    E002 --> E008\n165\t\n166\t    E003 --> E009\n167\t    E005 --> E009\n168\t    E006 --> E009\n169\t    E007 --> E009\n170\t    E008 --> E009\n171\t\n172\t    BA -.-> E006\n173\t    BA -.-> E007\n174\t    BA -.-> E008\n175\t    BA -.-> E009\n176\t    BA10 -.-> E009\n177\t    BA11 -.-> E009\n178\t    BB -.-> E006\n179\t    BB -.-> E007\n180\t    BB -.-> E008\n181\t    BB -.-> E009\n182\t    BB12 -.-> E009\n183\t    BB13 -.-> E009\n184\t```\n185\t\n186\t## DAG Justification\n187\t\n188\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n189\t|------|------|-----------|\n190\t| E001 → all six branch epics | 3 | Investigation selects the execution implementation; true gate (`remove` — the non-selected branch set is dropped). Bounded outcomes (2) → conditional branches. Each branch epic records `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E001`. |\n191\t| E002 → branch execution and persistence epics | 13 | The canonical result schema is a build-against artifact: run result records (execution epics) and the Model Registry write path (persistence epics) are written against it. |\n192\t| E003 → branch execution epics (BRANCH-*-E004) | 3 | Licensing outcome shapes the suite-delivery scope (bundled vs. customer fetch) inside the execution epics. This is a scope-shaping dependency, not a formal gate: `gated_by` is single-valued and holds the structural E001 gate on every branch epic, so the licensing outcome is consumed as an explicit acceptance criterion (suite-delivery scope finalized against the allowlist) rather than recorded as a second gate — keeping automated gate detection (`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`) unambiguous. |\n193\t| BRANCH-A-E004 → BRANCH-A-E010; BRANCH-B-E004 → BRANCH-B-E012 (within branch) | 12, 13 | The persistence capability writes the result records the execution capability produces; framework before consumer, within the same branch outcome. |\n194\t| BRANCH-A-E004 → BRANCH-A-E011; BRANCH-B-E004 → BRANCH-B-E013 (within branch) | 12 | Custom evaluation tasks run through the execution flow the execution epic provides; within the same branch outcome. |\n195\t| E002 → E005 | 13 | Validation is performed against the committed schema — a build-against artifact edge, harmonized with the E002 → E006/E007/E008 treatment (no `gated_by`). Version-bump resilience is the epic's acceptance criterion; if the schema fails it, the schema investigation revises the schema and the validation re-runs. |\n196\t| E001 → E006 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the backend contract the Llama Stack provider delegates to; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n197\t| E002 → E006 | 13 | Provider result representation builds against the canonical schema. |\n198\t| E001 → E007 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the resource shapes/API the Dashboard reads (LMEvalJob CR vs. new service CR/API); true gate (`rewrite`). |\n199\t| E002 → E007 | 13 | Score display and comparison build against the persisted result schema. |\n200\t| E001 → E008 | 3 | Engine outcome determines the API the pipeline step calls; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n201\t| E002 → E008 | 13 | Score outputs exposed by the step derive from the result schema. |\n202\t| E003 → E009 | 3, 11 | True gate: the docs' disconnected-environment guidance consumes the benchmark licensing allowlist (which suites ship in-product vs. customer fetch), so the licensing outcome rewrites that content. Recorded on E009 as `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9002-E003` with `gate_failure_impact` action `rewrite` (fallback: guidance rewritten against the final per-suite delivery decisions). |\n203\t| E005 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n204\t| E006 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n205\t| E007 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n206\t| E008 → E009 | 11 | docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics. |\n207\t| (dashed) branch execution epics → E006/E007/E008 | 12, 13 + branch rule | Framework → consumer coupling: consumers build against the selected execution implementation's API/resource surface. Because branch epics are not main-plan nodes, this coupling is enforced by acceptance criteria on E006/E007/E008 (\"platform evaluation API/resource shapes available and documented\") rather than frontmatter dependencies — mirroring the AC-gate pattern of Rule 10/13 for references. |\n208\t| (dashed) all branch epics → E009 | 11 + branch rule | Docs describe the implemented capability — including result persistence and custom task authoring — so every branch epic's outcome feeds the docs; enforced via the \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC since branch epics are not main-plan nodes. |\n209\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 (no edges) | 14 | Independent investigations with independent inputs; all three start immediately. |\n210\t\n211\tRules checked and not applicable: 5-10, 20, 25 (no onboarding/Konflux epics —\n212\tevery touched repo is already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context,\n213\texcept the BRANCH-B standalone-repo contingency, which is carried as an\n214\tAC-gated onboarding chain on the branch epic rather than a pre-created epic);\n215\t19 (not safety-critical); 22 (no off-platform infrastructure).\n216\t\n217\tCritical path: the longest frontmatter-only path is 3 epics (e.g.,\n218\tE002 → E005 → E009, or E001 → branch execution epic → branch persistence\n219\tepic); including the AC-gated branch-to-consumer coupling the effective path\n220\tis 4 epics (e.g., E002 → branch execution epic → E007 → E009).\n221\t`critical_path_length: 4` records the effective path.\n222\t\n223\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n224\t\n225\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n226\t|-----|----------|---------|\n227\t| HLR-1 Launch evaluations of served models | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 |\n228\t| HLR-2 Standard suites out of the box | P0 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (informed by E003) |\n229\t| HLR-3 Results persisted & linked to Model Registry version | P0 | BRANCH-A-E010 / BRANCH-B-E012 (write path); E005 (validation) |\n230\t| HLR-4 Custom evaluation tasks | P1 | BRANCH-A-E011 / BRANCH-B-E013 |\n231\t| HLR-5 Dashboard runs, scores, comparison | P1 | E007 |\n232\t| HLR-6 Evaluation as pipeline step | P1 | E008 |\n233\t| HLR-7 Llama Stack eval provider backed by platform | P1 | E006 |\n234\t| HLR-8 Cost/duration metadata | P2 | BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 (capture; schema fields defined in E002, persisted via the branch persistence epics) |\n235\t| HLR-9 End-to-end product documentation | P2 | E009 |\n236\t\n237\tFull coverage — no orphaned HLRs. HLR-4 (custom evaluation tasks) is carved\n238\tinto its own branch epics (P1, matching the HLR's priority) because it is an\n239\tadditive, independently deferrable capability on the execution flow — it can\n240\tbe planned and deferred without disturbing the P0 path. HLR-8 remains bundled\n241\twith the execution epics: its fields are defined by the E002 schema and\n242\tinterwoven with the run's result record, so splitting it out would duplicate\n243\tthe same result-record work (Step 4: tightly coupled lower-priority scope\n244\tstays bundled).\n245\t\n246\t## Conditional Branches\n247\t\n248\tE001 has two bounded outcomes; only one branch set materializes. Each\n249\toutcome's scope is split along the capability axis so every epic is a\n250\tsingle-sprint unit and the deferrable P1 custom-task work is planned\n251\tindependently:\n252\t\n253\t- **Outcome A — extend LMEvalJob** (three epics, all `gated_by` E001 with\n254\t  gate failure impact `remove` — each exists only if outcome A holds):\n255\t  - `BRANCH-A-E004` — execution core: promote the Tech Preview LMEvalJob\n256\t    path to the supported single execution path (launch, suite delivery,\n257\t    cost/duration metadata capture, backward compatibility).\n258\t  - `BRANCH-A-E010` — persistence: write result records to the Model\n259\t    Registry version of the evaluated model (P0, HLR-3 write path).\n260\t  - `BRANCH-A-E011` — custom evaluation tasks (P1, HLR-4; independently\n261\t    deferrable).\n262\t- **Outcome B — new evaluation service** (three epics, all `gated_by` E001\n263\t  with gate failure impact `remove`):\n264\t  - `BRANCH-B-E004` — service core: the new platform evaluation service\n265\t    wrapping lm-evaluation-harness, its API surface, suite delivery,\n266\t    cost/duration metadata capture, and the migration/coexistence plan for\n267\t    existing LMEvalJob users. If the investigation determines the service\n268\t    requires a standalone repository, the standard onboarding chain\n269\t    (repo-onboarding ∥ license-validation → konflux-onboarding →\n270\t    implementation) prepends to this epic — carried as an explicit\n271\t    AC/contingency.\n272\t  - `BRANCH-B-E012` — persistence: the service's Model Registry write path\n273\t    (P0, HLR-3 write path).\n274\t  - `BRANCH-B-E013` — custom evaluation tasks on the service's flow (P1,\n275\t    HLR-4; independently deferrable).\n276\t\n277\tBranch epics are attached to E001 as a plan document by the pipeline; they are\n278\texcluded from the main-plan critical path.\n279\t\n280\t## Health Warnings\n281\t\n282\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n283\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are absent (see parsing\n284\t  notes). Owner teams were inferred from architecture context — a human should\n285\t  confirm team assignments.\n286\t- **Docs epic priority**: E009 is P0 (docs-authoring exception: strategy\n287\t  priority `Critical` → P0) even though HLR-9 is P2. This is rule-compliant\n288\t  but reads as an inversion; flagged for human review.\n289\t- **Legal analysis on the P0 critical path**: E003 (licensing) gates the\n290\t  execution epics' out-of-the-box suite delivery. If legal turnaround is slow,\n291\t  a deferral option exists (ship the customer-fetch mechanism first and bundle\n292\t  allowlisted suites later) — noted here, not split into the DAG.\n293\t- **HLR-8 GPU-time metadata** depends on what the serving runtimes expose;\n294\t  partial availability is acceptable and captured as an AC on the execution\n295\t  epics rather than a separate epic.\n296\t- **Branch B repo contingency**: if the new service needs its own repository,\n297\t  the onboarding chain applies (noted on the branch epic; not pre-created\n298\t  since the outcome is undecided).\n299\t\n300\t## Tiered Delivery\n301\t\n302\tThe only externally-coupled work is E006 (Llama Stack eval provider), which\n303\tmay require an upstream contribution to `meta-llama/llama-stack` with\n304\tuncertain acceptance timing. Per Rule 21, a viable fallback exists (midstream\n305\tcarry / cherry-pick in `opendatahub-io/llama-stack-k8s-operator`), so it is\n306\trecorded as an AC on E006, not a separate epic:\n307\t\n308\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: core evaluation capability — execution and\n309\t  persistence (branch epics), persistence validation (E005), Dashboard views\n310\t  (E007), pipeline step (E008).\n311\t- **Deferrable**: the custom-task branch epics (P1, HLR-4) are additive on\n312\t  the execution flow and can be deferred without disturbing the P0 path —\n313\t  the reason they are separate epics.\n314\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E006 — Llama Stack provider, P1, blocks no\n315\t  other epic.\n316\t\n317\tNo other external dependencies have uncertain timing; Model Registry